                    <p class="notebook-p coming-soon-regular fw-bold underline" style="font-size:1rem;">Chinese Remainder Theorem</p>
                    <p class="notebook-p coming-soon-regular" >
                       Given the following set of linear congruences:<br>
                       x ≡ 2 (mod 5)<br>
                       x ≡ 3 (mod 11)<br>
                       x ≡ 5 (mod 17)<br>
                       Find the integer <span class="fw-bold">a</span> such that <span class="fw-bold">x ≡ a (mod 935)</span><br><br>
                    </p>
                    <p class="notebook-p coming-soon-regular dotted" style="background-color: rgba(151, 240, 42, 0.13);">
                        <span class="fw-bold highlight-green">Chinese Remainder Theorem</span> <br>
                        Let  <strong>m1,m2,…,mk</strong> be natural numbers such that each is greater than 1, and every pair of them is relatively prime.<br>
                        Let <strong>a1,a2,…,ak</strong> be integers.<br>
                        The system of congruences <br>
                        <strong>
                        x ≡ a<sub>1</sub> (mod m1)<br>
                        x ≡ a<sub>2</sub> (mod m2)<br>
                        .<br>
                        .<br>
                        .<br>
                        x ≡ a<sub>k</sub> (mod m<sub>k</sub>)<br>
                        </strong>
                        has a unique solution mod <strong>M</strong>, where <strong>M=m<sub>1</sub>m<sub>2</sub>⋯m<sub>k</sub></strong>
                    </p>
                    <p class="notebook-p coming-soon-regular">
                        <br>
                        <strong>Notatii:</strong><br>
                        <strong>M = m<sub>1</sub>·m<sub>2</sub>...m<sub>k</sub>​</strong><br>
                        <strong>M<sub>i</sub> = M/m<sub>i</sub></strong> - produsul tuturor modulelor, fara m<sub>i</sub><br>
                        <strong>y<sub>i</sub> = M<sub>i</sub><sup>-1</sup></strong> - inversa lui M<sub>i</sub> mod m<sub>i</sub>. <strong>M<sub>i</sub>·y<sub>i</sub> = 1</strong>
                        <br><br>
                        Solutia este data de <strong>x = ∑<sub>1</sub><sup>k</sup> a<sub>i</sub> · M<sub>i</sub> · y<sub>i</sub></strong><br>

                    </p>
                    <p class="notebook-p coming-soon-regular">
                        <br>
                        <strong>Explicatie:</strong><br>
                        Pentru mine aceasta teorema nu a avut sens fara teorema izomorfismului de inele:<br>
                        <span class="dotted font-red">
                            fie <strong>m<sub>1</sub>, m<sub>2</sub> ∈ N</strong> coprime. => <strong>ℤ/(m<sub>1</sub>·m<sub>2</sub>) ≅ ℤ/m<sub>1</sub> × ℤ/m<sub>2</sub></strong>
                        </span>
                        <br>Numerele trebuie sa fie coprime pentru a se pastra injectivitatea<br>
                        <br>
                        <strong>CRT</strong> se bazeaza pe acest lucru si defineste o functie <br>
                        <strong>ϕ:Z/(m1​m2​)⟶Z/m1​×Z/m2​</strong><br>
                        <strong>ϕ([x]m1​m2​​)=([x]m1​​, [x]m2​​)</strong><br>
                        De asemenea, exista definita si inversa acesteia:<br>
                        <strong>ϕ<sup>−1</sup>(a,b) = x ≡ ∑<sub>1</sub><sup>k</sup> a<sub>i</sub> · M<sub>i</sub> · y<sub>i</sub></strong> care este solutia cautata <br>
                    </p>

